  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "The sides (in centimeters) of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to eight times the rate of increase of the diagonal. What is the area $A$ of the square at that moment?\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{2}$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that\n$\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=8\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$\nso replacing $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 8\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 8 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, at that particular moment, we have $h=8,$ and therefore the area of the square at that moment is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{(8)^{2}}{2}=\\frac{64}{2}=32\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\n$$",
